#{[{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046644} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186516903} :activity/id #uuid "d6257b83-ba04-500d-a732-6cba09c74c43" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049479 :topic/id #uuid "f868b857-f4b2-5b40-8da2-046b9db36a57" :topic/math-academy-id 6721} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186516888 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186516768 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "69b3c7ed-0ad2-5549-acf8-acdbd9e0e4ea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-3e^{-4(x-1)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516341}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186516341} :answer-field/id #uuid "600c0667-9438-5a96-b5a3-42ddde81d0e1" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516690}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "07505f7b-3ca5-545d-9207-d1e1cfa4ebbe" :question/math-academy-id "q-343946" :question/problem "Consider the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+4y=f(x),\\,y(1)=-3\n$$\n\nwhere $f(x)$ is a $3$-periodic function. Fill in the missing part in the solution of the IVP, where the infinite sum on the right-hand side below represents a particular solution ${y}_{p}$ of the differential equation.\n\n*Hint: In this specific problem, the forcing function $f(x)$ is chosen such that the particular solution ${y}_{p}$ satisfies ${y}_{p}(1)=0.$*\n\nThe solution is \n\n$y(x)∼$   \n{{field-1}} \n$+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }({a}_{n}cos\\,(\\frac{2\\pi n}{3}x)+{b}_{n}sin\\,(\\frac{2\\pi n}{3}x))$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the general solution of a linear differential equation of the form ${y}^{′}+P(x)y=f(x)$ is given by\n\n$$\ny={y}_{c}+{y}_{p},\n$$\n\nwhere ${y}_{c}$ is the general solution of the corresponding homogeneous equation, and ${y}_{p}$ is a particular solution.\n\nFirst, we find the homogeneous solution. The general solution of ${y}^{′}+4y=0$ is \n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{-4x},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nNext, we identify the form of the general solution. Because the operator ${y}^{′}+4y$ involves a first derivative, a periodic forcing function $f(x)$ results in a particular solution containing both sine and cosine terms:\n\n$$\ny(x)∼A{e}^{-4x}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }({a}_{n}cos\\,(\\frac{2\\pi n}{3}x)+{b}_{n}sin\\,(\\frac{2\\pi n}{3}x))\n$$\n\nNow, we apply the initial condition $y(1)=-3.$ Using the hint that the particular solution part evaluates to zero at $x=1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(1) & =A{e}^{-4(1)}+{y}_{p}(1) \\\\ -3 & =A{e}^{-4}+0 \\\\ A & =-3{e}^{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $A$ back into the homogeneous part:\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=(-3{e}^{4}){e}^{-4x}=-3{e}^{4-4x}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is\n\n$$\ny(x)∼-3{e}^{4-4x}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }({a}_{n}cos\\,(\\frac{2\\pi n}{3}x)+{b}_{n}sin\\,(\\frac{2\\pi n}{3}x)).\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "3214bc2f-1faf-54eb-adf1-7d3eb074c726" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186516940}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186516899 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186516847 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "96e14832-25ec-5cc2-9b80-eef2d0a5f2d7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{5}x)(3-{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516425} {:answer/id #uuid "9a8746fb-2ea4-58ca-a484-de8db975ddb3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{5}x)(3+{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516429} {:answer/id #uuid "be7962f1-2c0b-553d-affa-31d3acba17bc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{5}x)({\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}-3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516514} {:answer/id #uuid "f9874a00-5d86-506a-b77b-4b0ce2fc6efe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{5}x)(3-{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516632}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186516425} :answer-field/id #uuid "8e8e4fce-3b23-5eb0-9715-6b625c2b4dc5" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516720}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "c1ec13b9-1623-5452-ac2a-c54844694d83" :question/math-academy-id "q-344034" :question/problem "$$\n{y}^{″}+5y=f(x),\\,y(0)=3,\\,{y}^{′}(0)=0\n$$\n\nConsider the initial value problem above, where $f(x)$ is an even, $3$-periodic function. Select the missing part in the solution of the IVP, where the second infinite sum on the right-hand side below represents a particular solution of the differential equation.\n\n$$\ny(x)∼{{field-1}}+{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}cos\\,(\\frac{2\\pi n}{3}x)\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the general solution of a linear differential equation of the form ${y}^{″}+{\\omega }^{2}y=f(x)$ is given by $y={y}_{c}+{y}_{p},$ where ${y}_{c}$ is the solution to the homogeneous equation and ${y}_{p}$ is a particular solution.\n\nFirst, we address the particular solution ${y}_{p}.$ Since $f(x)$ is an even function and the differential operator contains no ${y}^{′}$ (damping) term, the particular solution inherits the symmetry and is also an even function consisting only of cosine terms. This is represented by the term\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}cos\\,(\\frac{2\\pi n}{3}x).\n$$\n\nNext, we find the homogeneous solution. The characteristic equation ${\\lambda }^{2}+5=0$ gives roots $\\lambda =\\pm \\sqrt{5}\\text{i}.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{5}x)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{5}x).\n$$\n\nTo find the unknown constants, we apply the initial conditions. Differentiating the total solution gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}(x)∼-A\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{5}x)+B\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{5}x)-{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{2\\pi n}{3}{a}_{n}sin\\,(\\frac{2\\pi n}{3}x).\n$$\n\nUsing the derivative condition ${y}^{′}(0)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(0) & =-A\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)+B\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)-{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{2\\pi n}{3}{a}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0) \\\\ 0 & =0+B\\sqrt{5}-0 \\\\ B & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that because the total solution is an even function, its derivative is an odd function, which must vanish at $x=0.$\n\nNext, we use the condition $y(0)=3.$ With $B=0,$ the total solution is\n\n$$\ny(x)∼A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{5}x)+{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}cos\\,(\\frac{2\\pi n}{3}x).\n$$\n\nApplying $y(0)=3$ and noting that $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)=1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3 & =A+{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n} \\\\ A & =3-{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $A$ back into ${y}_{c}=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{5}x),$ we find the missing part:\n\n$$\ny(x)∼\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{5}x)(3-{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n})+{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}cos\\,(\\frac{2\\pi n}{3}x)\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "440bf108-473d-537f-bb47-60b7f6685da1" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186516910}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186516903 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186516829 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4ccc9c21-c3ce-5f4a-ba59-583860541dee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2e^{5x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516269}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186516269} :answer-field/id #uuid "9688ef1e-aa0f-557f-92e7-8f181132182a" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516724}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "a160ea33-0abc-551b-81fb-aa9297d75028" :question/math-academy-id "q-343758" :question/problem "Consider the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}-5y=f(x),\\,y(0)=-2\n$$\n\nwhere $f(x)$ is a $4$-periodic function. Fill in the missing part in the solution of the IVP, where the infinite sum on the right-hand side below represents a particular solution ${y}_{p}$ of the differential equation.\n\n*Hint: In this specific problem, the forcing function $f(x)$ is chosen such that the particular solution ${y}_{p}$ satisfies ${y}_{p}(0)=0.$*\n\nThe solution is \n\n$y(x)∼$  \n{{field-1}} \n$+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }({a}_{n}cos\\,(\\frac{\\pi n}{2}x)+{b}_{n}sin\\,(\\frac{\\pi n}{2}x))$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the general solution of a linear differential equation of the form ${y}^{′}+P(x)y=f(x)$ is given by\n\n$$\ny={y}_{c}+{y}_{p},\n$$\n\nwhere ${y}_{c}$ is the general solution of the corresponding homogeneous equation, and ${y}_{p}$ is a particular solution.\n\nFirst, we find the homogeneous solution. The general solution of ${y}^{′}-5y=0$ is \n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{5x},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nNext, we identify the form of the general solution. Because the operator ${y}^{′}-5y$ involves a first derivative, a periodic forcing function $f(x)$ results in a particular solution containing both sine and cosine terms:\n\n$$\ny(x)∼A{e}^{5x}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }({a}_{n}cos\\,(\\frac{\\pi n}{2}x)+{b}_{n}sin\\,(\\frac{\\pi n}{2}x))\n$$\n\nNow, we apply the initial condition $y(0)=-2.$ Using the hint that the particular solution part evaluates to zero at $x=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(0) & =A{e}^{5(0)}+{y}_{p}(0) \\\\ -2 & =A(1)+0 \\\\ A & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $A$ back into the homogeneous part:\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=-2{e}^{5x}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is\n\n$$\ny(x)∼-2{e}^{5x}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }({a}_{n}cos\\,(\\frac{\\pi n}{2}x)+{b}_{n}sin\\,(\\frac{\\pi n}{2}x)).\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "515e9c6d-b678-5d79-bcd7-3b888453f523" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186516888}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186516910 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186516813 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2e579e2a-cf55-5dc2-9631-36dd85e6533e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{5}\\pi x){\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{n{\\pi }^{3}(20-{n}^{2})}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516209} {:answer/id #uuid "69bd26b6-6e3c-5c38-a3bc-9ab6d641cd52" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{5}\\pi x){\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{10(-1)^{n}}{n{\\pi }^{2}(20-{n}^{2})}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516342} {:answer/id #uuid "987d4650-fafb-51a4-b9a2-850870587748" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{5}\\pi x){\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{2\\sqrt{5}(-1)^{n}}{n{\\pi }^{3}(20-{n}^{2})}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516427} {:answer/id #uuid "b57bb91e-bc6a-52e3-9e91-2b526a2371b1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{5}\\pi x){\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{20}{n{\\pi }^{2}(20-{n}^{2})}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516491}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186516427} :answer-field/id #uuid "ca20e363-d96f-5c7c-870a-e0d6761fc82a" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516742}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "6e09043a-2656-5006-b96f-e232ecdf95b0" :question/math-academy-id "q-344045" :question/problem "$$\n{y}^{″}+5{\\pi }^{2}y=f(x),\\,y(0)=0,\\,{y}^{′}(0)=0\n$$\n\nConsider the initial value problem above, where $f(x)$ is an odd, $4$-periodic function. Select the missing part in the solution of the IVP, where the second infinite sum on the right-hand side below represents a particular solution of the differential equation.\n\n$$\ny(x)∼{{field-1}}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\,\\frac{20(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{3}(20-{n}^{2})}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2})\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the general solution of a linear differential equation of the form ${y}^{″}+{\\omega }^{2}y=f(x)$ is given by $y={y}_{c}+{y}_{p},$ where ${y}_{c}$ is the solution to the homogeneous equation and ${y}_{p}$ is a particular solution.\n\nFirst, we address the particular solution ${y}_{p}.$ Since $f(x)$ is an odd function, its Fourier series consists only of sine terms. For a second-order equation with no ${y}^{′}$ (damping) term, the particular solution inherits this property and is also an odd function. This is represented by the term\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{20(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{3}(20-{n}^{2})}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2}).\n$$\n\nNext, we find the homogeneous solution. The characteristic equation ${\\lambda }^{2}+5{\\pi }^{2}=0$ gives roots $\\lambda =\\pm \\sqrt{5}\\pi \\,\\text{i}.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{5}\\pi x)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{5}\\pi x).\n$$\n\nTo find the unknown constants, we apply the initial conditions. Using $y(0)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(0) & =A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)+{y}_{p}(0) \\\\ 0 & =A(1)+0+0 \\\\ A & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that ${y}_{p}(0)=0$ because the particular solution is a sine series.\n\nNext, we use the derivative condition ${y}^{′}(0)=0.$ First, we compute the derivative of the total solution (with $A=0$):\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}(x)∼B\\sqrt{5}\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{5}\\pi x)+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{10(-1)^{n}}{n{\\pi }^{2}(20-{n}^{2})}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2})\n$$\n\nApplying ${y}^{′}(0)=0$ and noting that $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)=1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0 & =B\\sqrt{5}\\pi +{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{10(-1)^{n}}{n{\\pi }^{2}(20-{n}^{2})} \\\\ B\\sqrt{5}\\pi  & =-{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{10(-1)^{n}}{n{\\pi }^{2}(20-{n}^{2})} \\\\ B & =-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}\\pi }{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{10(-1)^{n}}{n{\\pi }^{2}(20-{n}^{2})} \\\\  & =-{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{10(-1)^{n}}{n\\sqrt{5}{\\pi }^{3}(20-{n}^{2})} \\\\  & =-{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{2\\sqrt{5}(-1)^{n}}{n{\\pi }^{3}(20-{n}^{2})}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $B$ back into ${y}_{c}=B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{5}\\pi x)$, we find the missing part:\n\n$$\ny(x)∼-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{5}\\pi x){\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{2\\sqrt{5}(-1)^{n}}{n{\\pi }^{3}(20-{n}^{2})}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\,\\frac{20(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{3}(20-{n}^{2})}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2})\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "600c0d6e-c648-5b58-bf0e-a4cd3e20ffb9"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186516940 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186516796 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0a99c937-0618-51b0-9ee8-a05c39b743bf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{2}x)(3-{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }n{b}_{n})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516132} {:answer/id #uuid "0d9a685b-e7bf-53bd-99ed-2a928b6f1ba2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{2}x)(\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{2}}-{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{n{b}_{n}}{\\sqrt{2}})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516142} {:answer/id #uuid "532771d5-519d-50af-94e8-f4b6987d74f4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{2}x)(\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{2}}-{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{n{b}_{n}}{\\sqrt{2}})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516288} {:answer/id #uuid "7c6ebad4-4e5c-5893-89b1-194838c5792a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{2}x)(\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{2}}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{n{b}_{n}}{\\sqrt{2}})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516376}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186516142} :answer-field/id #uuid "3b3e1afe-7ade-5f8c-9104-fa8e0533e940" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516673}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "465baa29-a84f-575d-bfbc-3ca094c0808a" :question/math-academy-id "q-343950" :question/problem "$$\n{y}^{″}+2y=f(x),\\,y(0)=0,\\,{y}^{′}(0)=3\n$$\n\nConsider the initial value problem above, where $f(x)$ is an odd, $2\\pi $-periodic function. Select the missing part in the solution of the IVP, where the second infinite sum on the right-hand side below represents a particular solution of the differential equation.\n\n$$\ny(x)∼{{field-1}}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the general solution of a linear differential equation of the form ${y}^{″}+{\\omega }^{2}y=f(x)$ is given by $y={y}_{c}+{y}_{p},$ where ${y}_{c}$ is the solution to the homogeneous equation and ${y}_{p}$ is a particular solution.\n\nFirst, we address the particular solution ${y}_{p}.$ Since $f(x)$ is an odd function, its Fourier series consists only of sine terms. For a second-order equation with no ${y}^{′}$ (damping) term, the particular solution inherits this property and is also an odd function. This is represented by the term\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx).\n$$\n\nNext, we find the homogeneous solution. The characteristic equation ${\\lambda }^{2}+2=0$ gives roots $\\lambda =\\pm \\sqrt{2}\\text{i}.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{2}x)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{2}x).\n$$\n\nTo find the unknown constants, we apply the initial conditions. Using $y(0)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(0) & =A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)+{y}_{p}(0) \\\\ 0 & =A(1)+0+0 \\\\ A & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that ${y}_{p}(0)=0$ because the particular solution is a sine series.\n\nNext, we use the derivative condition ${y}^{′}(0)=3.$ First, we compute the derivative of the total solution (with $A=0$):\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}(x)∼B\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{2}x)+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }n{b}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)\n$$\n\nApplying ${y}^{′}(0)=3$ and noting that $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)=1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3 & =B\\sqrt{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }n{b}_{n} \\\\ B\\sqrt{2} & =3-{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }n{b}_{n} \\\\ B & =\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{2}}-{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{n{b}_{n}}{\\sqrt{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $B$ back into ${y}_{c}=B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{2}x),$ we find the missing part:\n\n$$\ny(x)∼\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{2}x)(\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{2}}-{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{n{b}_{n}}{\\sqrt{2}})+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "9efa598a-3ff9-5f3e-ac3f-7a8b4ea4f3f9" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186516899}}] :activity/title "Solving IVPs Using Fourier Series" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516107}]}
